1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,096
GateReleaseService records timers into a
MeterRegistry. Restart a pod and in-
process counters reset. Scale to twelve
gate replicas and you have twelve private
notebooks.

2
00:00:12,215 --> 00:00:22,079
Prometheus scrapes each instance, stores
samples over time, and lets you ask what
p95 harbor.gate.release.duration was
across the fleet at 14:02.

3
00:00:22,199 --> 00:00:35,712
Boot exposes a scrape endpoint when
micrometer-registry-prometheus is on the
classpath and Actuator exposes it: Look at
this example. # gate-service A scrape
returns text like: Look at this text for a
moment.

4
00:00:35,832 --> 00:00:42,840
Micrometer turns dots into underscores and
appends _total for counters, _seconds for
timers.

5
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:53,183
If you curl the endpoint and do not see
harbor_gate_release_*, the meter never
registered or the scrape hits a different
pod than the one you exercised.

6
00:00:53,304 --> 00:01:08,424
Walk that check during deploy: port-
forward one gate pod, curl
localhost:8081/actuator/prometheus | grep
harbor_gate, confirm series before wiring
alerts. Wire Prometheus to the gate pods:
Look at this example.

7
00:01:08,543 --> 00:01:20,376
# prometheus.yml PromQL for gate latency
and error ratio: Look at this example. #
p95 release duration across gate pods Read
the queries carefully.

8
00:01:20,495 --> 00:01:33,311
rate needs a range at least several scrape
intervals — [5m] on a 15s scrape is sane;
[15s] flaps. Divide by started, not by a
raw counter without rate, or restarts look
like error spikes.

9
00:01:33,431 --> 00:01:48,095
histogram_quantile needs histogram buckets
(percentiles-histogram: true); a summary
cannot do accurate server-side p95 across
instances the same way. Sum by le before
quantile so you aggregate pods correctly.

10
00:01:48,215 --> 00:02:01,583
Timers in Micrometer become histograms or
summaries depending on config; prefer
histograms when you need
histogram_quantile. Protect the scrape
endpoint — it should not be world-readable
on the public gateway.

11
00:02:01,703 --> 00:02:12,839
Cardinality discipline from the Micrometer
episode applies here: one unbounded label
turns a scrape into a cost incident;
Prometheus will still try to ingest until
retention and memory hurt.

12
00:02:12,959 --> 00:02:23,471
Failure symptoms: scrape only one replica
via a mistaken Service monitor and the
dashboard lies about fleet health; alert
on absolute counter values and page every
process restart;

13
00:02:23,591 --> 00:02:29,279
expose /actuator/prometheus on the trucker
hostname and leak internal series.

14
00:02:29,399 --> 00:02:38,399
Another: up{job="harbor-gate"} == 0 after
a path rename to /actuator/metrics — wrong
path, silent gap in graphs.

15
00:02:38,519 --> 00:02:50,879
Trade-offs: shorter scrape intervals catch
spikes faster and cost more; longer
intervals miss brief booth storms.
Recording rules precompute expensive gate
queries for dashboards; they add
indirection.

16
00:02:50,999 --> 00:02:57,311
Keep raw series for forensics. Alerting
hygiene belongs next to scrape config.

17
00:02:57,431 --> 00:03:08,951
Page on up == 0 for the harbor-gate job
when all pods vanish; warn when a single
pod’s scrape fails while siblings succeed
— that is a pod or network partition, not
necessarily a fleet outage.

18
00:03:09,071 --> 00:03:20,927
Keep runbook links beside the PromQL so
03:10 does not start with reinventing
histogram_quantile. A misconception is
scraping only one gate pod and calling it
fleet health.

19
00:03:21,047 --> 00:03:31,175
Another is alerting on raw counter values
instead of rates. A third is exposing
/actuator/prometheus through the trucker-
facing gateway without auth.

20
00:03:31,295 --> 00:03:42,671
Raw PromQL in a black terminal is powerful
and miserable during a 3am booth backup.
Operators need panels, shared time ranges,
and a dashboard that already knows the
gate queries.

21
00:03:42,791 --> 00:03:53,471
Save the exploratory PromQL for
engineering; promote the survivors into
Grafana and recording rules once they have
earned a page. That shared picture is
Grafana.
